{"ts": 1791256539.246346, "type": "step", "step": 16, "action": "messages_send_to_student", "args": {"text": "You\u2019re stuck on the eval part, so here\u2019s the key distinction to use in the writeup: **pass@k** means \u201cat least one of k runs succeeds,\u201d while **pass^k** means \u201call k runs succeed.\u201d If the build asks about reliability, you usually want the stricter one. Try to answer this in your own words: why would pass^k be a better fit than pass@k for a deployment-style claim?", "intent": "hint", "concept": "week3-evaluation"}, "output": "Sent.\nStudent replied: ok\n[activity] t=0 messages 2m", "t": 2, "activity": [{"t": 0, "app": "messages", "minutes": 2, "detail": ""}], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.296727034588242, "pomdp": 0.296727034588242, "pass_k": 0.35185260979528166}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0, "pomdp": 192.0, "pass_k": 192.0}, "attention": 0.6327447761339733, "motivation": 0.44783718433757524, "reliance": 0.33294461262462643, "persistence": 0.4671650147483821, "persona": {"learn_rate": 0.28910681059556054, "slip": 0.1, "guess": 0.2, "fatigue_rate": 0.02, "distraction_rate": 0.3019095011780912, "type": "answer_seeking"}, "off_task_streak": 0, "feed_minutes": 0.0, "phone_minutes": 0.0, "nag": 1.0, "seen_posts": []}}
